I'll carefully analyze all the provided paper analyses to synthesize a comprehensive field guide. Let me first review what we have in detail, then construct the strategic document.

Based on my thorough analysis of the 6 fully-detailed paper analyses (MathVista, MMMU, MathVerse, MATH-Vision, Math-LLaVA, AlphaGeometry2) and the context of the broader 21-paper corpus in multi-modal mathematical reasoning, here is the Field Guide:

```json
{
  "innovation_patterns": [
    {
      "pattern": "Diagnostic Taxonomy Benchmarking",
      "description": "Constructing benchmarks organized around multi-dimensional taxonomies (reasoning type × task type × visual context × difficulty level) that don't just measure aggregate performance but expose specific, actionable failure modes. The taxonomy itself becomes a contribution that frames future research targets. Papers succeed when they show not just THAT models fail but precisely WHERE — enabling the community to focus effort.",
      "examples": ["MathVista (7 reasoning × 5 task × 19 visual contexts)", "MMMU (30 subjects × 6 disciplines × error taxonomy)", "MATH-Vision (16 disciplines × 5 difficulty levels × 6-category error taxonomy)", "MathVerse (DI/IP/EC information-type decomposition × 6 controlled versions)"],
      "success_rate": "high"
    },
    {
      "pattern": "Controlled Modality Ablation",
      "description": "Systematically removing or isolating one modality (text, vision, symbolic) to prove that models exploit shortcuts rather than performing genuine multimodal integration. The key move is creating matched problem variants where the same underlying question requires different modality reliance, transforming a vague suspicion into a falsifiable experimental protocol. The counterintuitive finding (e.g., models do BETTER without visual input) becomes a compelling headline result.",
      "examples": ["MathVerse (6 versions from text-dominant to vision-only)", "MATH-Vision (image ablation showing GPT-4 drops to 8.16% without images)", "Math-LLaVA (text-only inference diagnostic: 32.4% vs 46.6%)"],
      "success_rate": "high"
    },
    {
      "pattern": "Data-Centric Scaling on Frozen Architectures",
      "description": "Rather than proposing novel architectures, dramatically improving performance through data engineering — mining more Q&A pairs per image, augmenting along orthogonal axes (complexity, consistency, underspecification), and using GPT-4V as a synthesis oracle. This pattern works because the community often over-indexes on architectural novelty while the binding constraint is training signal density and diversity.",
      "examples": ["Math-LLaVA (MathV360K: 19-point gain via data alone, no architectural changes)"],
      "success_rate": "medium"
    },
    {
      "pattern": "Neuro-Symbolic Interface Enrichment",
      "description": "Instead of replacing symbolic reasoning with neural networks OR using neural networks as mere auxiliary-point proposers, enriching the information passed between neural and symbolic components — giving the LM forward-chaining facts, backward-chaining hypotheses, AND numerical heuristic signals simultaneously. The key insight is that the interface between neural and symbolic components is the bottleneck, not either component alone.",
      "examples": ["AlphaGeometry2 (S₁/S₂/S₃ analysis strings, SKEST cross-tree knowledge sharing)"],
      "success_rate": "high"
    },
    {
      "pattern": "Contamination-Resistant Source Curation",
      "description": "Sourcing evaluation data from naturally contamination-resistant sources — competition problems with copyright protection, college exams without easily-crawlable answers, or expert-curated content requiring STEM graduate students. This addresses the meta-problem that synthetic or web-scraped benchmarks are absorbed into training corpora faster than they can be evaluated against.",
      "examples": ["MATH-Vision (19 math competitions, <1% n-gram overlap at n≥6)", "MMMU (college exams selected for non-crawlability)", "MathVista (leaderboard with hidden test labels)"],
      "success_rate": "high"
    },
    {
      "pattern": "Failure-Mode-Driven System Improvement",
      "description": "Systematically categorizing failure modes of a predecessor system, then designing targeted improvements for each failure category rather than making monolithic changes. This produces papers with exceptionally clean ablations because each improvement maps to a specific diagnosed problem.",
      "examples": ["AlphaGeometry2 (3 failure modes → 3 targeted fixes: domain language expansion, DDAR2 C++ rewrite, SKEST search)"],
      "success_rate": "high"
    }
  ],
  "reviewer_preferences": [
    "Actionable granularity over monolithic scores: Reviewers consistently reward papers that show WHERE models fail (GPT-4V at 77% on function plots but 21.6% on logical reasoning puzzles — MathVista) rather than just reporting aggregate numbers. The fine-grained taxonomy IS the contribution.",
    "Proactive contamination analysis: Reviewers will demand evidence of contamination resistance — MATH-Vision's Reviewer 3 specifically requested n-gram overlap testing, and the authors' delivery of <1% at n≥6 was crucial for acceptance. Papers without contamination analysis are increasingly rejected.",
    "Counterintuitive headline findings: MathVerse's finding that models score HIGHER without diagrams, MATH-Vision's 53-point human-model gap, and AlphaGeometry2's finding that tokenizer choice doesn't matter all became signature results. Reviewers reward findings that challenge community assumptions.",
    "Clean component ablations: Math-LLaVA's isolation of selection, mining, complexity, consistency, and underspecification contributions earned praise. AlphaGeometry2's ablation of each improvement against a shared baseline demonstrates individual contribution. Reviewers are skeptical of systems where improvements cannot be decomposed.",
    "Shortcut/overfitting diagnostics: Math-LLaVA's text-only inference test, MathVerse's six-version protocol, and MATH-Vision's image ablation all proactively address the obvious reviewer concern: 'Are you measuring what you think you're measuring?' Papers that anticipate this critique outperform those that don't.",
    "Scale AND rigor: MMMU's 50+ annotators with multi-stage QC, MathVista's 99.2% inter-annotator agreement, and MATH-Vision's 4-stage quality pipeline show that reviewers want both large datasets and credible quality assurance. Scale without rigor gets rejected; rigor without scale gets relegated to workshops.",
    "Crossing psychologically meaningful thresholds: AlphaGeometry2 surpassing IMO gold medalists (42/50 vs 40.9/50) transforms a quantitative improvement into a milestone. Reviewers reward papers that mark clear before/after moments for the field.",
    "Rebuttal preparedness: MathVista's authors added 67 pages of GPT-4V analysis during revision; MATH-Vision addressed visual necessity concerns by showing only 2.89% truly lack visual context. The strongest papers have authors who treat review as an opportunity to strengthen the work, not just defend it."
  ],
  "field_gaps": [
    {
      "gap": "Disentangling visual perception errors from mathematical reasoning errors",
      "evidence": "MathVista reviewers flagged this as a fundamental limitation — when a model fails on a geometry problem, is it because it can't see the diagram or can't do the math? MATH-Vision's error taxonomy (42.2% reasoning vs 31.9% vision recognition) provides approximate decomposition but uses post-hoc categorization rather than a principled separation. MathVerse's DI/IP/EC framework approaches this but doesn't fully resolve it.",
      "difficulty": "high",
      "impact_if_solved": "high"
    },
    {
      "gap": "Math-specific vision encoders for domain-specific visual languages",
      "evidence": "MathVerse asks explicitly: 'Can we build math-specific vision encoders rather than general-purpose CLIP/ViT that actually improve performance on vision-dominant problems?' MMMU shows GPT-4V approaches random chance on chemical structures, music sheets, and circuit diagrams. Current vision encoders are trained on natural images and have no geometric or symbolic visual priors.",
      "difficulty": "high",
      "impact_if_solved": "high"
    },
    {
      "gap": "Genuine multimodal fusion vs. modality-sequential processing",
      "evidence": "MMMU found text-only GPT-4 with OCR/captions (31.9%) barely differs from random, suggesting current vision-language fusion is not truly 'joint.' MathVerse proved most models score higher WITHOUT visual input. AlphaGeometry2's pure LLMs score 0/50 on geometry. The field has no architecture that demonstrably performs cross-modal reasoning rather than processing modalities sequentially.",
      "difficulty": "high",
      "impact_if_solved": "high"
    },
    {
      "gap": "Chain-of-thought rationales for multimodal mathematical training data",
      "evidence": "Math-LLaVA explicitly identifies the absence of intermediate reasoning steps in synthesized Q&A pairs as their primary limitation. MATH-Vision found CoT degrades performance for some models (Gemini Pro -3.32%, Qwen-VL-Max -2.96%), suggesting current CoT implementations are poorly calibrated for multimodal settings. No large-scale dataset exists with step-by-step visual-mathematical rationales.",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "impact_if_solved": "high"
    },
    {
      "gap": "Dynamic difficulty scaling to prevent benchmark saturation",
      "evidence": "MathVista's open questions explicitly raise 'How should benchmarks handle the rapid obsolescence problem?' MATH-Vision's leaderboard shows GPT-5.4 at 96.1% with Python tools by mid-2026, suggesting shelf-life is limited. MathVista reviewers noted findings were 'already being superseded at review time.' No principled methodology exists for creating benchmarks that remain challenging as models improve.",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "impact_if_solved": "medium"
    },
    {
      "gap": "Cross-domain transfer of neuro-symbolic search techniques",
      "evidence": "AlphaGeometry2's SKEST mechanism has no evidence of cross-domain transfer despite the authors' claims. The question is explicitly open: can cross-tree knowledge sharing generalize beyond geometry to combinatorics, algebra, or program synthesis? No paper has attempted to transfer AG2's architecture to non-geometric mathematical domains.",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "impact_if_solved": "high"
    },
    {
      "gap": "Principled curriculum design for visual-mathematical training",
      "evidence": "Math-LLaVA found a 2:3:4:1 complexity ratio empirically but never theoretically motivated it. No paper provides a principled framework for optimal difficulty distribution in visual math training data. The field lacks curriculum learning theory adapted to multimodal mathematical reasoning.",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "impact_if_solved": "medium"
    },
    {
      "gap": "Multi-diagram and multi-step visual reasoning",
      "evidence": "MathVerse explicitly restricts scope to single-diagram problems. No benchmark or method handles problems requiring synthesis across multiple visual inputs (e.g., comparing two graphs, referencing a diagram while reading a table). Real mathematical reasoning frequently requires integrating multiple visual sources.",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "impact_if_solved": "high"
    },
    {
      "gap": "Compute efficiency of neuro-symbolic systems",
      "evidence": "AlphaGeometry2 uses TPUv4 clusters, 300M synthetic theorems, and multiple model replicas — the paper never reports total compute budgets. Whether similar results can be achieved with orders-of-magnitude less compute is entirely unknown, limiting accessibility and reproducibility of the approach.",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "impact_if_solved": "medium"
    }
  ],
  "untried_combinations": [
    {
      "idea_sketch": "MathVerse's DI/IP/EC information taxonomy as a data synthesis framework: use the decomposition to generate curriculum-aware training data where each image produces versions with progressively more information shifted to the visual channel, explicitly training models to extract visual information rather than relying on textual redundancy.",
      "from_papers": ["MathVerse", "Math-LLaVA"],
      "why_promising": "MathVerse diagnosed the modality-reliance problem but offered no solution. Math-LLaVA showed data-centric approaches close large gaps. Combining MathVerse's information taxonomy with Math-LLaVA's multi-axis augmentation strategy would create training data that explicitly penalizes textual shortcutting — the diagnosed disease meets the proven treatment delivery mechanism."
    },
    {
      "idea_sketch": "SKEST-style knowledge sharing for multimodal reasoning: adapt AlphaGeometry2's cross-tree fact-sharing mechanism to general MLLM inference, where multiple reasoning chains (visual, symbolic, textual) run in parallel and share intermediate conclusions, creating a fusion mechanism that goes beyond attention-based integration.",
      "from_papers": ["AlphaGeometry2", "MathVerse", "MMMU"],
      "why_promising": "MMMU and MathVerse both identify that current fusion is 'modality-sequential' rather than truly joint. SKEST shows that parallel reasoning processes can share intermediate results productively. Applying this to general multimodal math reasoning (not just geometry) could create the first architecture that demonstrably integrates modalities rather than processing them sequentially."
    },
    {
      "idea_sketch": "Competition-sourced problems + synthetic multi-version augmentation: take MATH-Vision's naturally calibrated competition problems and apply MathVerse's six-version methodology to create a training dataset where each competition problem has controlled modality-reliance variants, then train with Math-LLaVA's pipeline.",
      "from_papers": ["MATH-Vision", "MathVerse", "Math-LLaVA"],
      "why_promising": "Competition problems provide authentic difficulty and contamination resistance (MATH-Vision). The six-version methodology provides controlled modality-reliance variants (MathVerse). Math-LLaVA proved data-centric approaches work. Combining all three creates training data that is simultaneously high-quality, vision-demanding, and difficulty-calibrated — properties no existing dataset has together."
    },
    {
      "idea_sketch": "Analysis strings for general MLLMs: extend AlphaGeometry2's S₁/S₂/S₃ (forward-chaining, backward-chaining, numerical heuristic) information packaging beyond geometry to general visual math problems, giving the LM structured multi-signal inputs rather than flat image-text concatenation.",
      "from_papers": ["AlphaGeometry2", "MathVista", "MATH-Vision"],
      "why_promising": "AG2's analysis strings are the most sophisticated neural-symbolic interface in the literature but are restricted to geometry. MathVista's taxonomy reveals 7 reasoning types that could each benefit from structured intermediate signals. MATH-Vision's error analysis shows 42.2% of errors are reasoning failures — exactly the kind that forward/backward chaining signals could address. No one has tried bringing this structured-input paradigm to general multimodal math."
    },
    {
      "idea_sketch": "Error-taxonomy-guided adaptive evaluation: combine MMMU's perception/knowledge/reasoning error taxonomy with MathVerse's controlled ablation to create an adaptive benchmark that automatically routes models through increasingly harder modality-isolating tests to pinpoint failure modes without human annotation.",
      "from_papers": ["MMMU", "MathVerse", "MATH-Vision"],
      "why_promising": "All three benchmarks provide error taxonomies but require expensive human annotation to categorize errors. By combining MathVerse's automated modality ablation with MMMU's category framework, you could create a self-diagnosing benchmark that automatically identifies whether a failure is perceptual, knowledge-based, or inferential — solving the disentanglement problem that MathVista reviewers flagged as fundamental."
    },
    {
      "idea_sketch": "Synthetic geometry pretraining for general vision encoders: use AlphaGeometry2's 300M synthetic theorem pipeline to pretrain or fine-tune vision encoders on geometric diagrams with dense symbolic annotations, then transfer to general visual math reasoning.",
      "from_papers": ["AlphaGeometry2", "MathVerse", "Math-LLaVA"],
      "why_promising": "MathVerse shows vision encoders actively hurt geometric reasoning. AG2 has a proven pipeline for generating unlimited geometric training data with formal annotations. Math-LLaVA shows fine-tuning on domain-specific data closes large gaps. Yet no one has used AG2's synthetic data pipeline to improve general-purpose vision encoders for mathematical diagrams — a straightforward but potentially high-impact transfer."
    }
  ],
  "method_building_blocks": [
    {
      "tool": "DI/IP/EC Information Decomposition",
      "what_it_does": "Classifies each piece of information in a multimodal problem into Descriptive Information (redundant with diagram), Implicit Properties (inferable from diagram), or Essential Conditions (numerical values), enabling controlled ablation of modality reliance.",
      "used_by": ["MathVerse"],
      "could_be_applied_to": "Training data curation for any multimodal task — by tagging which information comes from which modality, you can create curriculum schedules that progressively force models to rely more on visual input. Also applicable to scientific diagram understanding, medical imaging QA, and architectural plan interpretation."
    },
    {
      "tool": "SKEST (Shared Knowledge Ensemble of Search Trees)",
      "what_it_does": "During parallel beam searches with diverse configurations, intermediate facts proven by one failing search branch are written to a shared database and injected into other trees' symbolic engines, turning partial failures into collaborative successes.",
      "used_by": ["AlphaGeometry2"],
      "could_be_applied_to": "Any multi-agent or ensemble reasoning system where intermediate results have standalone validity — code synthesis (proven lemmas), multi-step word problems (verified sub-calculations), scientific hypothesis testing (confirmed experimental constraints). Could also improve tree-of-thought and graph-of-thought prompting by sharing verified intermediate nodes."
    },
    {
      "tool": "Three-Stage Evaluation Protocol (Generate → LLM-Extract → Normalize-Score)",
      "what_it_does": "Separates answer generation, answer extraction (via GPT-4 with >99.5% accuracy), and scoring into independent stages, eliminating brittle regex-based extraction while maintaining deterministic scoring.",
      "used_by": ["MathVista"],
      "could_be_applied_to": "Any benchmark with heterogeneous answer formats (free-form text, numbers, equations, multiple choice). Particularly valuable for open-ended scientific reasoning benchmarks, code generation evaluation, and any domain where model outputs are unpredictably formatted."
    },
    {
      "tool": "Analysis Strings (S₁/S₂/S₃ Fact Sets)",
      "what_it_does": "Packages three complementary signals for the language model: S₁ (facts deducible from premises via forward chaining), S₂ (facts deducible assuming the goal via backward chaining), and S₃ (facts numerically true in the diagram), giving the LM multiple reasoning anchors simultaneously.",
      "used_by": ["AlphaGeometry2"],
      "could_be_applied_to": "General mathematical reasoning systems — any setting where forward reasoning from givens, backward reasoning from goals, and empirical/numerical verification provide complementary signals. Could transform how MLLMs receive structured mathematical context beyond flat text concatenation."
    },
    {
      "tool": "GPT-4V-Bootstrapped Data Synthesis with TF-IDF/K-Means Diverse Demonstrations",
      "what_it_does": "Clusters existing questions by TF-IDF features to select maximally diverse few-shot demonstrations, then uses GPT-4V to generate new Q&A pairs conditioned on images, followed by three-axis augmentation (complexity, consistency, underspecification).",
      "used_by": ["Math-LLaVA"],
      "could_be_applied_to": "Any domain where images are information-rich but existing Q&A pairs underexploit them — medical imaging (one scan, many diagnostic questions), satellite imagery analysis, materials science microscopy, or any domain with expensive-to-annotate visual data."
    },
    {
      "tool": "N-gram Contamination Analysis",
      "what_it_does": "Measures n-gram overlap between benchmark examples and known model training corpora at varying n values (n=3 through n=8), with <1% at n≥6 as the acceptance threshold for contamination resistance.",
      "used_by": ["MATH-Vision"],
      "could_be_applied_to": "Any new benchmark paper — this is rapidly becoming a required analysis for benchmark acceptance at top venues. Should be applied proactively to both existing and new benchmarks, especially those curated from web-accessible sources."
    },
    {
      "tool": "Complexity-Weighted Sampling",
      "what_it_does": "Assigns sampling weights to training examples based on complexity level (e.g., 2:3:4:1 for easy:medium:hard:expert), biasing training toward harder examples as a form of implicit curriculum learning.",
      "used_by": ["Math-LLaVA"],
      "could_be_applied_to": "Any instruction-tuning pipeline — particularly relevant for mathematical reasoning where difficulty levels are naturally graded. Could be extended with dynamic scheduling (increasing difficulty over training) rather than static ratios."
    },
    {
      "tool": "Cross-Benchmark Inconsistency Analysis",
      "what_it_does": "Compares model rankings across multiple benchmarks to detect contamination — if Model A beats Model B on Benchmark X but not Benchmark Y, and the tasks are similar, this suggests one benchmark may be contaminated.",
      "used_by": ["MATH-Vision"],
      "could_be_applied_to": "Meta-evaluation of any benchmark family. Particularly valuable as the number of benchmarks proliferates — could become a standard diagnostic tool for benchmark validity rather than an ad hoc analysis."
    },
    {
      "tool": "KL Divergence / Total Variation Validation for Dev-Test Splits",
      "what_it_does": "Quantitatively validates that a development/mini-test subset faithfully represents the full test set's distribution across all taxonomy dimensions, using KL divergence (target: <0.01) and Total Variation (target: <0.04).",
      "used_by": ["MathVista"],
      "could_be_applied_to": "Any benchmark with a public development set and hidden test set — ensures that performance on the public subset is a reliable predictor of performance on the private set, which is critical for leaderboard-based evaluation."
    },
    {
      "tool": "Two-Phase CoT Scoring with Information Hiding",
      "what_it_does": "Phase 1: GPT-4 extracts N key reasoning steps from the model's output WITHOUT seeing the question or answer (preventing evaluator bias). Phase 2: GPT-4V scores each step against ground truth. Final score weights intermediate reasoning (70%) against answer correctness (30%).",
      "used_by": ["MathVerse"],
      "could_be_applied_to": "Any evaluation where process matters as much as product — mathematical proof evaluation, scientific reasoning assessment, programming problem solving, or any domain where correct answers can be reached via incorrect reasoning."
    }
  ],
  "common_pitfalls": [
    "Claiming multimodal reasoning when models exploit textual redundancy: MathVerse proved most MLLMs score HIGHER without diagrams. Any paper claiming improved visual math reasoning MUST include modality ablation experiments (text-only baselines, image removal tests) — papers without this diagnostic will be rejected by informed reviewers.",
    "Treating benchmarks as static targets: MATH-Vision's leaderboard shows GPT-5.4 at 96.1% with tools by mid-2026. Benchmark papers that don't address longevity (difficulty scaling, dynamic generation, evolving contamination resistance) risk being obsolete before review completion.",
    "Ignoring contamination risks: MATH-Vision's Reviewer 3 demanded n-gram contamination testing; MathVista uses a hidden test set. Any benchmark paper submitted without contamination analysis is vulnerable to a devastating reviewer critique that is trivially predictable.",
    "Reporting aggregate metrics without diagnostic decomposition: All four benchmark papers (MathVista, MMMU, MathVerse, MATH-Vision) earned top-venue acceptance specifically because their taxonomies enabled fine-grained failure analysis. A paper reporting only 'our method achieves X% on MathVista' without per-category breakdowns provides insufficient signal for the community.",
    "Conflating model architecture contribution with data contribution: Math-LLaVA showed a 19-point gain from data alone. Papers proposing new architectures must ablate against the possibility that their gains come from better/more training data rather than the architecture itself. Failure to do so invites the devastating 'is this just a data paper?' critique.",
    "Overfitting to a single benchmark: MATH-Vision showed model rankings are inconsistent across benchmarks (InternLM 12.4% higher than GPT-4V on MathVista but 7.8% lower on MATH-V). Papers must evaluate on multiple benchmarks (MathVista + MathVerse + MATH-V + MMMU at minimum) to demonstrate genuine capability rather than benchmark-specific optimization.",
    "Assuming chain-of-thought always helps: MATH-Vision found CoT degrades Gemini Pro by 3.32% and Qwen-VL-Max by 2.96%. Papers that adopt CoT without empirically validating it helps for their specific model-task combination are making an unjustified assumption.",
    "Underspecifying human baselines: MathVista's 60.3% human accuracy was questioned by reviewers as potentially reflecting annotation quality or time-pressure artifacts rather than genuine human difficulty. Papers must carefully document human baseline methodology (annotator qualifications, time constraints, interface design) to make human-model comparisons interpretable.",
    "Neglecting open-source accessibility: MMMU noted closed-source models significantly outperform open-source ones but resist contamination analysis. Papers that evaluate only closed-source APIs limit reproducibility and community trust. Always include open-source model evaluations.",
    "Publishing geometry-only results and claiming general mathematical reasoning: AlphaGeometry2 is brilliant but explicitly limited to Euclidean geometry. Papers must be honest about scope and avoid overclaiming when their approach targets a specific mathematical subdomain."
  ],
  "field_trajectory": "The field of multi-modal mathematical reasoning is converging on three macro-directions that will likely dominate through 2027. First, the BENCHMARK ERA IS ENDING and the METHOD ERA IS BEGINNING: MathVista, MMMU, MathVerse, and MATH-Vision collectively mapped the problem space comprehensively between 2024-2025, but rapid model improvement (GPT-5.4 at 96.1% on MATH-V with tools) is saturating static benchmarks. The next high-impact contributions will be methods that demonstrably solve the diagnosed problems — particularly genuine visual-symbolic integration rather than textual shortcutting — evaluated against the diagnostic frameworks these benchmarks established. Second, NEURO-SYMBOLIC HYBRID ARCHITECTURES with enriched interfaces (following AlphaGeometry2's paradigm of multi-signal analysis strings and cross-tree knowledge sharing) will likely outperform end-to-end neural approaches for complex mathematical reasoning, but the key unsolved challenge is making these architectures general-purpose rather than domain-specific. The paper that successfully transfers AG2-style structured reasoning interfaces from geometry to general multimodal math will be a landmark. Third, DATA-CENTRIC APPROACHES with vision-aware curriculum design will be the most accessible and immediately impactful research direction: Math-LLaVA showed pure data engineering closes large gaps, and combining this with MathVerse's modality-reliance insights to create training data that forces genuine visual understanding (rather than rewarding textual shortcutting) is the clearest path to near-term progress. The overarching trajectory is a shift from 'can models do multimodal math?' (answered: poorly) to 'how do we build systems that genuinely integrate visual, symbolic, and textual reasoning?' — and the winners will be papers that demonstrate integration through principled diagnostics (modality ablation, information decomposition, error taxonomy) rather than just aggregate accuracy improvements.",
  "meta_advice": "If you are a PhD student aiming to publish a strong 2026 paper in multi-modal mathematical reasoning, here is what the evidence says. First, DO NOT build another benchmark — the field has four major ones (MathVista, MMMU, MathVerse, MATH-Vision) that collectively cover the evaluation space, and reviewers will demand you justify why yours is necessary rather than additive. Instead, build a METHOD that addresses a specific failure mode diagnosed by existing benchmarks — MathVerse's finding that models score higher without diagrams is the most actionable target. Second, your paper's credibility will live or die on its ablation protocol: include modality ablation (text-only baselines), component ablation (each contribution isolated), cross-benchmark evaluation (minimum 3 benchmarks), and contamination awareness. Math-LLaVA succeeded at EMNLP Findings largely because every component was cleanly ablated and the text-only overfitting diagnostic was proactively included. Third, consider the data-centric path seriously — it requires less computational overhead than architectural innovation and Math-LLaVA proved it works. The most promising untried combination is using MathVerse's DI/IP/EC information decomposition as a framework for generating training data that explicitly forces visual reliance, trained with Math-LLaVA's augmentation pipeline. This would address a diagnosed problem (textual shortcutting) with proven tools (data synthesis) in a novel combination. Fourth, if you pursue architectural innovation, study AlphaGeometry2's interface design philosophy: the key is not 'neural vs. symbolic' but 'what information do you pass between components and in what form?' Analysis strings, SKEST knowledge sharing, and structured multi-signal inputs are deeply underexplored outside geometry. Finally, your paper needs a headline finding — something counterintuitive, dramatic, or threshold-crossing. MathVerse's 'models do better without diagrams,' MATH-Vision's '53-point human-model gap,' and AlphaGeometry2's 'surpassing IMO gold medalists' are the findings that made these papers memorable and citable. Design your experiments to surface surprising patterns, not just incremental improvements. The field rewards papers that change how we think about the problem, not just how well we solve it."
}
